WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.870
Have you ever clicked a chart and watched the whole page change?

2
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.226
That is filter flow.

3
00:00:06.226 --> 00:00:10.974
In this lesson, you will learn how filters move across relationships, when to use

4
00:00:10.974 --> 00:00:14.704
single or both directions, and how to handle many to many.

5
00:00:14.704 --> 00:00:16.400
Let's see it in action!

6
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.491
Relationships decide how filters move.

7
00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:23.997
On this page, we have two table visuals:

8
00:00:23.997 --> 00:00:28.380
Order IDs from Order Details, and Product Names from Products.

9
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.544
In Model view, Products and Order Details are joined on Product ID.

10
00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.521
It is many to one:

11
00:00:46.521 --> 00:00:51.660
each product appears many times in Order Details, but only once in Products.

12
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.852
Back on the report, watch this.

13
00:01:07.852 --> 00:01:12.982
I select a product name, and the Order ID table shows only that product's orders.

14
00:01:12.982 --> 00:01:16.744
The filter flows from the one side to the many side.

15
00:01:16.744 --> 00:01:19.480
That is the magic of a good model!

16
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.937
With a Single direction, filters flow only one way.

17
00:01:36.937 --> 00:01:41.817
You can choose Both, but use it with care, because it can slow your

18
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:43.560
model and give confusing results.

19
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.931
Big models get busy. Here's a tip:

20
00:02:09.931 --> 00:02:14.192
click the plus sign at the bottom of Model view to add a new

21
00:02:14.192 --> 00:02:17.540
layout, and drag in only the tables you want to study.

22
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.925
Here I add All Customers and North Customers.

23
00:02:45.925 --> 00:02:49.440
Customer ID appears more than once in both tables.

24
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.625
So Power BI sets many to many, and shows a warning.

25
00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:06.146
It is allowed, but duplicate keys often mean your data needs cleaning first.

26
00:03:06.146 --> 00:03:08.580
Now you know what to look for!

